You should be very comfortable designing:
FSMs (Moore/Mealy)
Counters
Pipelines
Handshake protocols
FIFOs
Clock domain crossing basics

Languages:
Verilog
SystemVerilog (preferred)

Key concepts interviewers test:
blocking vs non-blocking
latch inference
timing behavior
reset strategies
synthesizable coding style

2️⃣ FPGA Architecture Understanding
Many candidates know RTL but don’t understand FPGA internals.
You should know basics of:
LUTs
Flip-flops
DSP blocks
BRAM
routing fabric
clocking resources

Example interview question:
“How does a multiplier map onto FPGA resources?”
Understanding this shows hardware awareness.

3️⃣ Timing & Constraints (Very Important)
Many RTL engineers struggle here.
You should understand:
setup and hold time
timing closure
critical paths
pipelining for timing
multi-cycle paths
false paths

Tools used:
Xilinx Vivado
Intel Quartus

4️⃣ Verification Mindset
Writing RTL is only half the job.

You should know how to verify designs using:
self-checking testbenches
assertions (SVA)
constrained random testing

Advanced verification:
UVM

But don’t rush into UVM without fundamentals.

Important components:
drivers
monitors
scoreboard
coverage
Even simple structured verification already puts you ahead of many candidates.

5️⃣ Debugging Skills (Extremely Valuable)
Good engineers are debuggers.

You should know how to debug using:
waveform analysis
simulation logs
assertions
FPGA debug tools

For FPGA debugging:
Integrated Logic Analyzer (ILA)
Chipscope / Vivado debugger

Example scenario:
“Why is FIFO empty flag wrong?”
The interviewer wants to see your thought process.








